Explanation

This is not a certificate or path issue anymore — your manual run shows all certs are fine.

The problem is systemd cannot find or access the config or keys correctly because the OpenVPN systemd service runs as root but expects config/key files in /etc/openvpn or proper permissions.

On Ubuntu 24:

OpenVPN systemd expects server config in /etc/openvpn/server/

Keys under /etc/openvpn/server/ or readable by root

If your keys are in /root/openvpn-ca/pki/ (home directory), systemd may fail due to AppArmor restrictions or root home not mounted early.

Solution 1: Move keys to /etc/openvpn/server

Create a folder for keys:

sudo mkdir -p /etc/openvpn/server/keys

Copy the keys and certs:

sudo cp /root/openvpn-ca/pki/ca.crt /etc/openvpn/server/keys/
sudo cp /root/openvpn-ca/pki/issued/server.crt /etc/openvpn/server/keys/
sudo cp /root/openvpn-ca/pki/private/server.key /etc/openvpn/server/keys/
sudo cp /root/openvpn-ca/pki/dh.pem /etc/openvpn/server/keys/
sudo cp /etc/openvpn/ta.key /etc/openvpn/server/keys/

Adjust server.conf paths:

ca /etc/openvpn/server/keys/ca.crt
cert /etc/openvpn/server/keys/server.crt
key /etc/openvpn/server/keys/server.key
dh /etc/openvpn/server/keys/dh.pem
tls-auth /etc/openvpn/server/keys/ta.key 0

Ensure root owns everything:

sudo chown -R root:root /etc/openvpn/server/keys
sudo chmod 600 /etc/openvpn/server/keys/*
Solution 2: Test systemd again
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start openvpn-server@server
sudo systemctl status openvpn-server@server

This should now start successfully.

✅ Why this works

Manual run works because you are root in your home folder.

systemd enforces AppArmor and strict paths, so keys in /root/ are not guaranteed to be accessible.

Moving everything to /etc/openvpn/server/keys is the recommended Ubuntu 24 way.

